## Build the local polynomial

The standard Euler denominator of parameters $\alpha_i$ is $\prod_i(1-\alpha_iX)$. The following algorithm constructs it coefficient by coefficient.

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal why the central character is cubed</summary>
The original central character is $ab$. The lifted product is $(a^2)(ab)(b^2)=a^3b^3=(ab)^3$. This argument uses only commutative multiplication.
</details>

{{demo:1}}

## See the tensor decomposition

The tensor-square list is $(a^2,ab,ab,b^2)$. One copy of $ab$ belongs to the symmetric square and one to the determinant line. Explore the resulting factorization.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the complete factorization argument</summary>
The multiset $(a^2,ab,ab,b^2)$ is the union, with multiplicity, of $(a^2,ab,b^2)$ and $(ab)$. A union of parameter lists concatenates the factors $1-\alpha X$, so its Euler denominator is the product of the two denominators.
</details>

## Trace as a cubic divided difference

The lifted trace $a^2+ab+b^2$ obeys $(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2)=a^3-b^3$. The landscape below shows this positive-definite quadratic form over real inputs.

